{% extends "doc_page.jinja" %}
{% import "chrome.jinja" as chrome %}
{% import "docs_nav.jinja" as docsnav %}
{% set active_nav = 'docs' %}
{% block page_title %}Configuration - Netsuke Build System{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    <!-- Main Content Area with Sidebar -->
    <div class="site-container pt-16 grow flex">

        {{ docsnav.sidebar('configuration', sub_links=[{'href': '#configuration', 'label': 'Layers and keys'}, {'href': '#policy-exceptions', 'label': 'Security exceptions'}, {'href': '#budget-keys', 'label': 'Budget keys'}, {'href': '#diagnostics', 'label': 'Diagnostics'}]) }}

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <main id="main-content" class="hm-docs-content flex-1 min-w-0 py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-12">
{{ docsnav.mobile_docs_bar('configuration') }}

            {{ chrome.breadcrumb([{'href': '/netsuke/docs/', 'label': 'Docs'}, {'label': 'Configuration'}]) }}

            {% call chrome.page_header('Reference', 'Configuration') %}
                Configuration layers belong to the CLI, the environment, and config files rather than hidden defaults: one discovered base file &lt; project <code>.netsuke.toml</code> and its <code>extends</code> chain &lt; environment variables &lt; CLI flags. Fetch grants, <code>env()</code> allow rules, and evaluation budgets are the deliberate exceptions.
            {% endcall %}

            <section id="configuration" class="mb-20">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:settings-adjust" aria-hidden="true"></span> Configuration
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    When neither <code>--config</code> nor <code>NETSUKE_CONFIG</code> selects a file, Netsuke layers configuration in this order, with later entries overriding earlier ones:
                    <strong>one discovered base winner (user configuration, otherwise system configuration, otherwise built-in defaults) &lt; project <code>.netsuke.toml</code> and its <code>extends</code> chain &lt; <code>NETSUKE_</code> environment variables &lt; CLI flags</strong>.
                    Automatic discovery picks exactly one base file rather than merging system and user scopes, then appends the project layer so project values can override it while fields present only in the winner remain available. An explicit selector bypasses discovery entirely: the selected file replaces the discovered base and project layers, and environment variables and flags still apply on top. A flag on the command line always wins, and project config files can be overridden per-invocation without editing them. Three security-sensitive settings do not follow this order; see <a href="#policy-exceptions" class="hm-link">security exceptions</a>.
                </p>

                <div class="bg-warning/5 border border-warning/30 rounded-lg p-4 flex items-start mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    <span class="iconify text-warning mt-0.5 mr-3 shrink-0" data-icon="carbon:warning-alt-filled" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong class="text-base-content">Note:</strong> The keys below mirror <code>docs/sample-netsuke.toml</code> in the repository as of v{{ netsuke_version }}.
                        Netsuke is pre-1.0 &mdash; some key names and default values may still change before a stable release.
                        Check the <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke" class="hm-link">project repository</a> for the latest status.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm flex items-center">
                            <span class="iconify mr-2 text-primary" data-icon="carbon:document" aria-hidden="true"></span> Config file discovery
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mb-3">An explicit selector bypasses discovery entirely. Selectors are checked in this order:</p>
                        <ol class="space-y-1 text-xs font-mono text-charcoal-mid list-decimal list-inside">
                            <li><code>--config /path/to/file.toml</code> (CLI flag)</li>
                            <li><code>NETSUKE_CONFIG=/path/to/file.toml</code> (environment)</li>
                        </ol>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-3">Without a selector, automatic discovery chooses one exclusive base winner from the platform's user scope (XDG base directories and the home directory on Unix; <code>%APPDATA%\netsuke\config.toml</code> on Windows), then the system scope, then built-in defaults, and appends <code>.netsuke.toml</code> from the project directory selected by <code>-C/--directory</code>. Discovery runs once and is cached for the rest of the command.</p>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-3">CLI-only controls such as <code>--config</code>, <code>--directory</code>, and subcommands are not read from configuration files. <code>--directory</code> does not rebase a relative <code>--config</code> or <code>NETSUKE_CONFIG</code> path; pass an absolute selector when it must not depend on the invoking directory.</p>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-3">When discovery finds no file, Netsuke uses its built-in defaults. When it finds a file that cannot be loaded &mdash; malformed TOML, for example &mdash; it reports the load error rather than treating the broken file as absent. An explicit <code>--config</code> or <code>NETSUKE_CONFIG</code> selection that is missing or invalid is likewise an error; Netsuke does not fall back to discovery.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm flex items-center">
                            <span class="iconify mr-2 text-primary" data-icon="carbon:parameter" aria-hidden="true"></span> Environment variables
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mb-3">Settings can be controlled via <code>NETSUKE_</code>-prefixed env vars:</p>
                        <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs font-mono">
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_JOBS</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">8</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_LOCALE</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">es-ES</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_PROGRESS</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">never</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_ACCESSIBILITY</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">on</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_EMOJI</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">never</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_JSON</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">1</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_NO_INPUT</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">true</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_DEFAULT_TARGETS__0</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">hello.txt</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_NINJA</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">/opt/ninja/bin/ninja</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_WINDOWS_SHELL</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">bash</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_WHICH_WORKSPACE</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">0</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_TRUST_PROJECT_FETCH_POLICY</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">true</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_ENV_ALLOW_VAR</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">'["CI"]'</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_ENV_BLOCK_VAR</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">'["GITHUB_TOKEN"]'</code></div>
                            <div><code class="text-success-text">NETSUKE_MANIFEST_FUEL</code>=<code class="text-warning-text">50000000</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-3">Policy values are matched case-insensitively. <code>NETSUKE_JSON</code> accepts <code>true</code>/<code>1</code> and <code>false</code>/<code>0</code>; anything else is a validation error rather than a silent fallback. <code>NETSUKE_NINJA</code> overrides the Ninja executable used by <code>build</code> and <code>clean</code>; empty or non-UTF-8 values fall back to <code>ninja</code> on <code>PATH</code>. <code>NETSUKE_WHICH_WORKSPACE</code> set to <code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, or <code>off</code> switches off the explicit <code>cwd_mode="workspace-recursive"</code> search; it no longer governs a default fallback, because <code>which</code> has none. The two <code>NETSUKE_ENV_*_VAR</code> variables take serialized JSON arrays, not bare names.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Each key group's label is paired with its extract. Without
                     JavaScript every extract is shown, labelled by its heading;
                     config-keys.js turns the labels into tabs on narrow screens
                     and into emphasis toggles on wide ones. -->
                <div class="hm-config-keys mb-8" data-config-keys>
                    <h3 class="hm-config-keys__title" id="config-keys-title">Config keys</h3>
                    <div class="hm-config-keys__layout">
                        <div class="hm-config-keys__labels" data-config-keys-labels aria-labelledby="config-keys-title">
                            <div class="hm-config-keys__key" data-config-keys-key="build">
                                <span class="hm-config-keys__label" id="config-keys-label-build" data-config-keys-label="build">Build</span>
                                <p class="hm-config-keys__note" id="config-keys-note-build" data-config-keys-note="build">Where the manifest lives and how hard Netsuke works: the path to the Netsukefile, how many jobs may run at once, and what a bare <code>netsuke build</code> should make.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="hm-config-keys__key" data-config-keys-key="output">
                                <span class="hm-config-keys__label" id="config-keys-label-output" data-config-keys-label="output">Output</span>
                                <p class="hm-config-keys__note" id="config-keys-note-output" data-config-keys-note="output">What Netsuke prints, and how. Progress, accessible mode, emoji, and colour each auto-detect the terminal; set one explicitly to override the guess. <code>locale</code> picks the language messages are written in.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="hm-config-keys__key" data-config-keys-key="policy">
                                <span class="hm-config-keys__label" id="config-keys-label-policy" data-config-keys-label="policy">Policy</span>
                                <p class="hm-config-keys__note" id="config-keys-note-policy" data-config-keys-note="policy">What the <code>fetch()</code> and <code>env()</code> helpers may reach. Choose whether unlisted hosts are denied, name the hosts to allow or block, and list the environment variables a manifest may read. Allow entries belong in operator configuration: a project file cannot grant them on its own authority.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="hm-config-keys__panels" data-config-keys-panels>
                            <div class="hm-config-keys__panel" id="config-keys-panel-build" data-config-keys-panel="build" role="group" aria-labelledby="config-keys-label-build">
{% highlight 'toml' %}
# Keys are flat; this mirrors docs/sample-netsuke.toml
# Manifest path override
file = "Netsukefile"
# Number of parallel jobs (1-64)
jobs = 8
# Default build targets when the CLI names none
default_targets = ["fmt", "lint", "test"]
# Netsuke never prompts; `no_input = false` is rejected
no_input = true
{% endhighlight %}
                            </div>
                            <div class="hm-config-keys__panel" id="config-keys-panel-output" data-config-keys-panel="output" role="group" aria-labelledby="config-keys-label-output">
{% highlight 'toml' %}
# Output policies
progress = "auto"       # auto | always | never
accessibility = "auto"  # auto | on | off
emoji = "auto"          # auto | always | never
color = "auto"          # auto | always | never (colour rendering not yet implemented)
locale = "en-US"
# One versioned JSON document per command; disables tracing on stderr
json = false
# Diagnostic tracing, timing summaries, and a final metrics snapshot
verbose = false
{% endhighlight %}
                            </div>
                            <div class="hm-config-keys__panel" id="config-keys-panel-policy" data-config-keys-panel="policy" role="group" aria-labelledby="config-keys-label-policy">
{% highlight 'toml' %}
# Network policy for the fetch() helper
fetch_default_deny = false
# Allow entries in a project .netsuke.toml are ignored
# unless the operator enables trust_project_fetch_policy
fetch_allow_scheme = ["https"]
fetch_allow_host = ["*.example.com"]
fetch_block_host = ["metadata.internal"]
# Operator layers only; a project cannot set it for itself
trust_project_fetch_policy = false
# env() access: exact names, a block always wins
env_allow_var = ["CI", "PACKAGE_REGISTRY_TOKEN"]
env_block_var = ["AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"]
{% endhighlight %}
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="policy-exceptions" class="mb-20 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-6">
                    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content flex items-center">
                        <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:security" aria-hidden="true"></span> Security exceptions to precedence
                    </h2>
                    <span class="text-xs font-mono text-success-text bg-success/10 px-2 py-1 rounded">Since v0.1.0-beta4</span>
                </div>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    A checkout's own <code>.netsuke.toml</code> is a request, not an order. For three kinds of setting, the primary project file can tighten what the operator allows but never loosen it. System and user configuration, <code>NETSUKE_</code> variables, and CLI flags are the operator layers. Files pulled in through <code>extends</code> keep ordinary file-layer precedence for fetch and <code>env()</code> policy.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Fetch policy</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Project <code>fetch_allow_scheme</code> and <code>fetch_allow_host</code> are ignored unless an operator sets <code>trust_project_fetch_policy</code>. A project may turn <code>fetch_default_deny</code> on but not off, and its block entries always count. <a href="/netsuke/docs/security/#network-policy" class="hm-link">Network policy</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm"><code>env()</code> access</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Project <code>env_allow_var</code> entries are removed before the policy is composed, so they can neither widen the allowlist nor switch on default-deny. Project <code>env_block_var</code> entries accumulate. <a href="/netsuke/docs/security/#env-access" class="hm-link">Environment access</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Evaluation budgets</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A project value, including one from its <code>extends</code> chain, may lower a <code>manifest_*</code> ceiling but cannot raise one set by the defaults, user configuration, the environment, or the CLI. <a href="/netsuke/docs/security/#resource-budgets" class="hm-link">Resource budgets</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="budget-keys" class="mb-20 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-6">
                    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content flex items-center">
                        <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:meter" aria-hidden="true"></span> Budget keys
                    </h2>
                    <span class="text-xs font-mono text-success-text bg-success/10 px-2 py-1 rounded">Since v0.1.0-beta4</span>
                </div>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Seven keys bound manifest evaluation. They are not in <code>docs/sample-netsuke.toml</code>; the names and defaults below come from the upstream <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/users-guide.md#resource-budgets" class="hm-link">users' guide</a> and the CLI definition. Each has a <code>NETSUKE_</code> variable (the key in upper case) and a <code>--manifest-*</code> flag. Values must be positive integers.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-4" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'toml' %}
# Defaults shown; set these in trusted configuration
manifest_evaluation_fuel = 1000000           # MiniJinja instructions per evaluation
manifest_fuel = 100000000                    # MiniJinja instructions per manifest
manifest_rendered_value_bytes = 1048576      # 1 MiB per rendered value
manifest_rendered_manifest_bytes = 16777216  # 16 MiB across all rendered values
manifest_source_bytes = 4194304              # 4 MiB of template source
manifest_foreach_cardinality = 10000         # values per foreach
manifest_expanded_entries = 50000            # targets and actions after expansion
{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">A breach reports <code>Manifest resource budget exhausted</code> with the stage and limit, under the JSON code <code>netsuke::runner::manifest_budget_exceeded</code>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="diagnostics" class="mb-20 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:debug" aria-hidden="true"></span> Diagnose configuration selection
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Pass <code>--verbose</code> to see how Netsuke chose its configuration. Structured events on stderr report whether
                    <code>--config</code>, <code>NETSUKE_CONFIG</code>, or automatic discovery won, which environment lookups were attempted,
                    and each layer as the merge applies it. File-layer events carry a bounded <code>path_hash</code> rather than the raw path;
                    CLI events record only the overridden leaf keys, never their values. A rejected setting is reported with its <code>key</code>
                    and a bounded <code>reason</code>.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm">What a broken file does</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A discovered file that cannot be loaded &mdash; malformed TOML, or an <code>extends</code> parent that is missing &mdash; is reported as a load error, not treated as absent. An explicit selector that is missing or invalid is likewise an error; Netsuke never falls back to discovery from an explicit file. The warning records <code>failure_kind</code> as <code>Missing</code> or <code>LoadError</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm">Metrics snapshot</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">With <code>--verbose</code>, Netsuke emits one final <code>metrics snapshot</code> debug event before exiting: bounded counters and histograms for configuration loading (<code>config_load_total</code>, <code>config_load_duration_seconds</code>), cached discovery, raw CLI path validation, and legacy recipe execution. Since v0.1.0-beta4 it also carries <code>netsuke_runner_manifest_structures_total</code>, which counts manifest structural summaries and records only integer collection sizes, and <code>netsuke_manifest_env_lookups_total</code>, which counts <code>env()</code> lookups by <code>outcome</code> (<code>success</code>, <code>blocked</code>, <code>not_present</code>, <code>not_unicode</code>) without naming a variable. It is an in-process diagnostic record, not a metrics endpoint. JSON mode suppresses all of this tracing so stderr stays a single machine-readable document.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Help Footer -->
{{ docsnav.docs_footer('configuration') }}

        </main>
    </div>

    <script defer src="/netsuke/assets/vendor/minisearch.js"></script>
    <script defer src="/netsuke/assets/js/doc-search.js"></script>
    <script defer src="/netsuke/assets/js/config-keys.js"></script>

{% endblock %}
